CAUSE (kpi-detail.html, attempt 2 only): the `{{if and (eq .Type "healthcare")
.HealthcareNoCoverageInRange}} / {{else}} / {{end}}` wrapper on the Per Month
tile, the `{{$noCoverage := ...}}` local, and the `{{if $noCoverage}}` wrapper on
the vs-Avg <td> are written on their own lines WITHOUT `{{-`/`-}}` trim markers,
so their surrounding newline+indent text nodes are emitted for EVERY kind.
Sample (expenses, `diff -u | cat -A`):
                     <p class="text-sm ...">Per Month</p>$
   +                    $
                     <p class="text-xl font-bold ...">$1,850.00</p>$
   +                    $
   ...
                     <td class="... text-red-600 ...">$1,800.00</td>$
   +                        $
                     <td class="... text-green-700 ...">-2.7%</td>$
   +                        $

MATERIALITY (facts, for the lead's adjudication — I am not ruling on it):
  CMD: tr -d ' \t\n\r' < X | md5sum, master vs branch
  OUT: expenses 1cea4dfc...==1cea4dfc... ; income 005b6da8...==005b6da8... ;
       savings 8ebf07be...==8ebf07be... ; savings-rate d154e018...==d154e018...
  CMD: python3 html.parser token-stream compare, whitespace-only text nodes
       dropped, tags+attrs+entities+data compared in order
  OUT: expenses DOM-token-equal ntok=121/121 ; income 121/121 ;
       savings 132/132 ; savings-rate 132/132
  CMD: cmp on the CSV exports for the four existing kinds
  OUT: all four byte-identical.
So every non-whitespace byte and the whole DOM are unchanged, and the added
whitespace lands only between block <p> siblings and between <td>/<tr>
siblings, where HTML parsing discards it. The defect is real against the written
words ("byte-identical" / Design 7 "Existing kinds' markup byte-identical") and is
a regression from attempt 1, which met it exactly (KD1.1.checker-tests: "cmp
branch.dump master.dump -> TWIN-DUMP IDENTICAL, both files 47185 bytes"). It is
also cheap to fix (trim markers, or put each `{{if}}/{{else}}/{{end}}` inline).
No command I can run shows the criterion met, so I report it as not met.

